A||N||14||Lance||Head gasket?!?||||9||
Z||000000||Lance||02-19-2000||07:51 PM||||I think my head gasket is out. The problem is that I get some light blue smoke sometimes when I'm at an idle. And I have some oil coming out the exhaust. This is the head gasket right? If so, how big of a job is it to fix, or should I take it to someone? ||||reg||9
Z||000001||fiero56||02-19-2000||09:18 PM||||Rings most likely. Head gasket will allow oil in the antifreeze, and antifreeze in the oil. Oil coming out the exhaust is most likely piston rings. It could also be a problem with valves not sealing properly. ]<BR>Do a compression check on all cylinders(I-4 or V-6?). They should all be close to each other. If you do have one or more cylinders giving really low compression, stick a few drops of oil in the sparkplug hole, and see if the compression goes up. If it does, it's rings, if it doesn't it's valves.<P>It is possible it is the headgasket, but you would notice either steam in the exhaust, or antifreeze in the oil. Antifreeze is an anti-lubricant, if you find it in the oil, get it out fairly quickly.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Luperman||02-19-2000||09:51 PM||||you can also check to see if your oil is a milky color looking at the dipstick it is another indication that you have a blown head gasket or cracked block mainly due to overheating the engine, but the compression check is by far  more accruate<p>[This message has been edited by Luperman (edited 02-19-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000003||tiacPaul||02-19-2000||11:31 PM||||Blue smoke is always oil.  When my head blew water/antifreeze poured out of the muffler.  This would also likeky end you engine, because antifreeze ruins the bearings.  The oil would also turn white if the head gasket was letting water mix with the oil.  Pray it's not the head gasket!  But I don't think it is.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||02-20-2000||12:19 AM||||Oil coming out the exhaust dose not mean bad oil rings. I could be worn valve guides and seals. I had an Iron Duke that had blue smoke coming out of the exhaust and it was just worn valve guides and seals. An engine with good rings will show a rise in vacuum of about 5hg on deceleration of the engine, bad rings will not.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Lance||02-20-2000||12:28 AM||||My oil isn't white or anything like that. Infact it looks good. I just use more then normal.I cant take it to my normal guy to look at it, He said to run it untill it dies and get a "better" car. Needless to say, he isn't my mechanic any more.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Lance (edited 02-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||02-20-2000||01:05 AM||||Lance, it sounds like maybe your engine is "just" getting old, kind of like me. My iron duke used a lot of oil too, I kept pouring it in knowing it was going to eventually give out, which it finally did, but it lasted longer than I thought it would. If you're considering having that engine rebuilt, don't wait too long. The longer you run a worn out engine, the more it'll cost you to rebuild it. Next time I'm in Kansas City, I'll bring my compression gauge and we'll check out your motor.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Lance||02-20-2000||01:10 AM||||ok, let me know and I'll get you some directions. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||02-20-2000||10:39 AM||||I think the most common cause of blue smoke from Iron Dukes (especially the early ones) is worn valve guides.  My 84 had 'em!<P>Try this:  have a friend follow you on a short drive.  Run it up near redline in 2nd gear and let it coast back down.  If your valve guides are bad, your friend should see no blue smoke while you accelerate, but lots while you're coasting back down.  You'll also get a brief but big blue puff of smoke when you first start your car - oil seeps past those worn guides, then gets burned on startup. Luckily, worn valve guides do NOT mean your engine's getting ready to die!  <P>If your rings are bad, you'll blow blue smoke all the time, your compression will be low, and you'll soon be investing in a rebuild or a new engine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||02-20-2000||11:51 AM||||Chad speaks wisely. Another clue that it might be bad rings is to check the air cleaner. If there is a puddle of oil down in the bottom, then you have blow-by, which usually means worn out rings. If you're using oil, check it regularly. No engine will last very long if you run them low.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Lance||02-22-2000||01:48 AM||||I do have oil at the bottom of the oil filter. This could be the rings right? does anyone know what the labor is going to cost me to get that fixed? It dont sound like something I would be able to do. No room where I live. Thanx for all your help guys.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000011||batboy||02-22-2000||02:12 AM||||Lance, usually if the rings are worn out, it's time for a complete rebuild. That can get kind of expensive. If funds are short, try finding a good low mileage used engine.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||02-24-2000||10:51 PM||||Lance, you never did say how miles you get before having to add a quart of oil.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Lance||02-25-2000||03:08 PM||||Batboy.... You wanted to know how many miles i have to drive before I had to add oil. Well its around 500 miles and it will be a quart low. Would this be the rings? A friend of mine has a 4 cilinder 2.5 l for $250. It is in an 94 car, not sure what kind of car though. Its not a front wheel drive but do you think it would work for my car?<P>Thanx ||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||02-25-2000||07:45 PM||||Well, it's most likely rings or valve guides/seals or both. The oil in the air cleaner points towards worn out rings. A compression check is the next step. I don't remember what year Fiero you have Lance. If it's a DIS 87-88 4 cylinder, then you probably can use a newer engine. If you have an older Fiero, then the ECM and engine wiring won't work. You have to use the donor car stuff. If you don't use a Fiero or a front wheel drive engine, you may have to do some modifying, I don't know about the 2.5, but on the 2.8 the rear wheel drive engines have the starter on the opposite side. Better check all that carefully. They made changes on that 2.5 engine almost every year. It's better to find another Fiero engine the same year except low miles with good oil pressure and good compression.||||reg||1
